.Dd 1 May, 2009
.Dt launchd 8 
.Os Darwin
.Sh NAME
.Nm launchd
.Nd System wide and per-user daemon/agent manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl D
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl S Ar SessionType
.Op Ar -- command Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm 
manages processes, both for the system as a whole and for individual users.
The primary and preferred interface to
.Nm
is via the
.Xr launchctl 1
tool which (among other options) allows the user or administrator to load and unload jobs.
Where possible, it is preferable for jobs to launch on demand based on criteria specified
in their respective configuration files.
.Pp
During boot 
.Nm
is invoked by the kernel to run as the first process on the system and to further bootstrap the rest of the system.
.Pp
You cannot invoke
.Nm
directly.
.Sh ENVIRONMENTAL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width -indent
.It Pa LAUNCHD_SOCKET
This variable is exported when invoking a command via the launchd command line. It informs launchctl how to find the correct launchd to talk to.
.El
.Sh NOTES
In Darwin, the canonical way to launch a daemon is through
.Nm launchd
as opposed to more traditional mechanisms or mechanisms provided in earlier versions of Mac OS X. These alternate methods should
be considered deprecated and not suitable for new projects.
.Pp
In the
.Nm launchd
lexicon, a "daemon" is, by definition, a system-wide service of which there is one instance for all clients. An "agent" is a service that runs on
a per-user basis. Daemons should not attempt to display UI or interact directly with a user's login session. Any and all work that involves interacting
with a user should be done through agents. 
.Pp
If you wish your service to run as a certain user, in that user's environment, making it a
.Nm launchd
agent is the ONLY supported means of accomplishing this on Mac OS X. In other words, it is not sufficient to perform a
.Xr setuid 2
to become a user in the truest sense on Mac OS X.
.Ss Darling private user-domain clients
The private liblaunch entry points
.Fn _launch_msg_user
and
.Fn _launch_remove_user_wait
select the caller's effective-UID launchd explicitly: obtain the root bootstrap
send right and then use
.Fn bootstrap_look_up_per_user
with a NULL service name.
They never change the process bootstrap port or environment.
The message entry point obtains that selected launchd's socket using its
.Fn vproc_mig_getsocket
RPC, rejects the system socket, and owns a separate connection for each call.
It does not use the process-wide launch_msg connection cache or trusted check-in
descriptor. Exchanges reuse liblaunch's serialization, send/receive and real
security-session attachment code; no session grant is fabricated when an audit
session port or the attachment RPC is unavailable.
The request tree is borrowed and may be enriched with security-session UUIDs;
the returned tree is owned by the caller.
NULL with errno denotes a client or IPC failure, while an owned errno reply
preserves launchd's result. Client bootstrap-to-errno categories are not a
byte-exact native ServiceManagement error mapping.
.Pp
The wait entry point sends
.Dv VPROC_MAGIC_UNLOAD_SIGNAL
through
.Fn vproc_mig_send_signal
on the selected user bootstrap port and preserves its raw kernel/bootstrap
status. The existing server holds the MIG reply until removal finishes for a
running job; polling for a missing label is not completion evidence.
All acquired bootstrap send rights and per-request connection state are released.
The private declarations have default visibility in launch_priv.h; the existing
CMake target is
.Li launch
and the installed image is
.Pa /usr/lib/system/liblaunch.dylib .
.Pp
Reuse provenance: these are extensions of Apple's existing 2005-2012
Apache-2.0 liblaunch.c implementation, retaining its license header.
Current Darling upstream
.Li 60ba801decee7a00782f74f6be4c8ffb013f79ff
and VibeDarling
.Li e76e5ed862e79656d02266a3608d89d5fd914557
were inspected at their exact src/launchd/liblaunch/liblaunch.c paths:
their launch_client_init, launch_msg_internal and reply callback bodies retain
the implicit global/environment-selected connection; neither supplies these
explicit-domain helpers.
The bootstrap_look_up_per_user body in upstream libbootstrap.c and
_vproc_send_signal_by_label body in Vibe libvproc.c were also inspected.
Vibe is a read-only implementation reference, not a build authority.
The existing legacy_ipc_request Mach server supports CheckIn only, so it is not
a submit/remove transport. No server or authorization-provider expansion is
implied by these client additions.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/System/Library/LaunchDaemons" -compact
.It Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents
Per-user agents provided by the user.
.It Pa /Library/LaunchAgents
Per-user agents provided by the administrator.
.It Pa /Library/LaunchDaemons
System-wide daemons provided by the administrator.
.It Pa /System/Library/LaunchAgents
Per-user agents provided by Mac OS X.
.It Pa /System/Library/LaunchDaemons
System-wide daemons provided by Mac OS X.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO 
.Xr launchctl 1 ,
.Xr launchd.plist 5 ,
